{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/control-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-1.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/control-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-1.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage B: reading the control theses (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief\nand the one packet you are given.\n\nEach item of your packet pairs a **ruler question** (a question written by hand for an earlier, frozen measurement, with its\nunit, due day and ladder of rungs) with a **judged question** the system wrote itself for a thesis it opened (its question, the\noutcome it looks at, due day, year and rungs). It also lists **reader B's decisive items**: the evidence an earlier reader, called\nreader B, cited for the frozen answer to the ruler question (each with its day, title, passage and meaning), and **the evidence\nshown to the judge**: every evidence entry the system's judge was shown for the judged question in any of its requests on or\nbefore 2026-07-14 (empty when there is none). You are not told either answer, and you do not judge which answer is right.\n\nFor each item, answer three questions.\n\n1. `same_question`: **yes** when the judged question asks what the ruler question asks: the same quantity measured the same way\n   (the same total, scope and unit: not a part of it, a ceiling on it, another quantity or another unit), or the same event,\n   for the same period. **no** otherwise. Wording may differ; a judged question that asks for more or less than the ruler's,\n   or for another period, is **no**.\n2. `same_due`: for an **event** (a yes-or-no question), **yes** only when the judged question's deadline is the ruler's\n   deadline. For a **figure**, **yes** when the two due days both mark the publication of the same period's figure (for\n   example, both the annual report of the same fiscal year, even if the two days differ); **no** otherwise.\n3. `items`: for **each** of reader B's decisive items, by its id, **shown** when its content is present in the evidence shown to\n   the judge (the same fact or figure, in any wording, from any source), **not_shown** when it is absent. Search the entries for\n   the figure or the fact; do not mark an item shown because a related item is.\n\nReturn one JSON object per item, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"control\": 3, \"same_question\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"same_due\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"items\": {\"i005\": \"shown\"|\"not_shown\", ...}, \"why\": \"one sentence naming what decides each no and each not_shown\"}`.\n\n## Third reader (only where the two readers differ)\n\nA third reader of each family is given this brief, the packet, and a list of the disputed points, each a control number and a\nfield (`same_question`, `same_due`, or `item <id>`), without the two readers' answers. For each point, answer that one question\nafresh by the rules above and return one JSON object per point, one per line, and nothing else:\n`{\"control\": 3, \"field\": \"item i005\", \"ruling\": \"yes\"|\"no\"|\"shown\"|\"not_shown\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}` (`yes`/`no` for the two\nquestion fields, `shown`/`not_shown` for an item).\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 1,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia make first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026, as confirmed in Nvidia's quarterly reports, filings, or management commentary?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"First customer shipments / initial revenue from Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by the company.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"Nvidia confirms that it made first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"After July 2, 2026, Nvidia formally cancels or abandons the Vera Rubin platform before making any first customer shipments. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is reported to be in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026), per Motley Fool analyst Justin Pope.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e42 [party_statement; Justin Pope (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for the Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\",\n   \"- e4 [fact; TradingKey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01] NVIDIA selected Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, an architecture decision the item frames as significant for the Rubin product cycle (the system is described as intended to power data centres for the next two to three years).\\n    Quote: NVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. … NVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next \",\n   \"- e42 [party_statement; Justin Pope (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for the Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e75; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e75 [party_statement; The Motley Fool (Bram Berkowitz); original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang, CEO) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in March that he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e86 [party_statement; Josh Parker (NVIDIA), reported by BGR; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA, in a blog post by its head of corporate sustainability Josh Parker, announced that its DSX AI factories are hosting its Rubin AI infrastructure in a fully liquid-cooled design with no fans or cold aisles; the design circulates liquid coolant that enters the chip at 45 degrees Celsius and exits at 55 degrees with processors running at full performance.\\n    Quote: In Nvidia's blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at Nvidia, it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles. … So, how does the Nvidia DSX design work? Liquid coolant enters the chip at 45 degrees Celsius, absorbs heat from across the chip's surface, and exits at 55 degrees. It was observed that processors\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is reported to be in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026), per Motley Fool analyst Justin Pope.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e101; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e101 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are to begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e107 [party_statement; Adria Cimino / fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia plans to launch its first-ever stand-alone CPU in fall 2026 as part of the Vera Rubin platform, aiming to compete in the roughly $200 billion CPU market where it has not previously been a major presence, and the article argues it could dominate the data-center CPU segment specifically. Reported by fool.com columnist Adria Cimino.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform. … I'll start with the goal, and this is to dominate the $200 billion CPU market -- a market where Nvidia has not been greatly present in the past. … I think the company could clearly dominate in the CPU market for data centers.\",\n   \"- e108 [party_statement; Adria Cimino / fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in 2026 ('this year'), per the article — a company revenue projection for its new stand-alone CPU line launched within the Vera Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e76; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is reported to be in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026), per Motley Fool analyst Justin Pope.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e101, e184; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e124 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the generation-over-generation increase.\\n    Quote: According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. … Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generatio\",\n   \"- e126 [party_statement; MarketScale; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 rack first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the item.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n   \"- e145 [rumour; Danielle Liverance (247wallst.com); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e179 [party_statement; Investing.com / Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's delay report, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e133 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \\\"manufacturing execution concerns\\\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\",\n   \"- e181 [party_statement; Jordan Klein (Mizuho) via Investing.com; original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co. Ltd 2026-07-03; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Nvidia supplier Hon Hai Precision Industry Co. Ltd, one of Nvidia's largest suppliers, reported strong earnings last Friday and its management forecast a continued acceleration in AI demand; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein called this \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\\n    Quote: The analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. … Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\",\n   \"- e184 [party_statement; TIKR.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Nvidia’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the article.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e194 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] The item reports that the Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack, and that Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e211 [party_statement; cnbc.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] Nvidia disputed a SemiAnalysis report that its next-generation Kyber server rack was running into delays, i.e., the company pushed back on the claim that its Kyber product schedule had slipped. The dispute came on a Monday, per CNBC.\\n    Quote: Similar action took place in Nvidia Monday after the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e194 (); both are in force: The item reports that the Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack, and that Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e246 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its more powerful Rubin line of processors in the second half of 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\",\n   \"- e259 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson (via futuriom.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson responded that \\\"Our road map is intact\\\" and that \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline,\\\" reaffirming the company's schedule in the face of the reported Kyber manufacturing setbacks.\\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e133 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \\\"manufacturing execution concerns\\\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\",\n   \"- e259 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson (via futuriom.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson responded that \\\"Our road map is intact\\\" and that \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline,\\\" reaffirming the company's schedule in the face of the reported Kyber manufacturing setbacks.\\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e308, e322; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e133 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \\\"manufacturing execution concerns\\\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\",\n   \"- e308 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nvidia Corporation ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied the speculation that Kyber is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the item states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e259; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e258 (); both are in force: This week, research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks. The article says this could be further impetus for NVIDIA to deploy its new credit and revenue-sharing model.\",\n   \"- e338 [party_statement; moomoo.com (relaying Nvidia's statement to Bloomberg); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Nvidia said in a recent statement to Bloomberg that it denies claims of a technical delay and that its product roadmap remains intact; the item does not specify which product or delay claim the denial addresses.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e258 (); both are in force: This week, research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks. The article says this could be further impetus for NVIDIA to deploy its new credit and revenue-sharing model.\",\n   \"- e107 [party_statement; Adria Cimino / fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia plans to launch its first-ever stand-alone CPU in fall 2026 as part of the Vera Rubin platform, aiming to compete in the roughly $200 billion CPU market where it has not previously been a major presence, and the article argues it could dominate the data-center CPU segment specifically. Reported by fool.com columnist Adria Cimino.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform. … I'll start with the goal, and this is to dominate the $200 billion CPU market -- a market where Nvidia has not been greatly present in the past. … I think the company could clearly dominate in the CPU market for data centers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e349; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e362 [fact; Quantum Zeitgeist; original source Perplexity ; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] AI company Perplexity is among the first to adopt NVIDIA's newly unveiled Vera data center CPU, designed to maximize single-threaded performance for agentic AI workloads, and Perplexity is planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is challenging a core tenet of modern data center design with Vera, a new CPU built to maximize single-threaded performance at scale, and AI innovator Perplexity is among the first to adopt it. … Perplexity is now planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems, demonstrating confidence in its ability to enhance agent performance and overall system efficiency.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e382 [party_statement; TIKR.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA highlighted its Vera CPU line as a new growth driver and said it expects the line to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, entering a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD. This is company guidance for the CPU product line, not a report of platform shipments.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e76; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e384 [fact; TIKR.com; original source Perplexity ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] AI startup Perplexity has chosen to use NVIDIA's new CPUs for AI agent tasks, a customer selection of the company's new CPU line.\\n    Quote: AI startup Perplexity has also chosen to use Nvidia’s new CPUs for AI agent tasks.\",\n   \"- e427 [party_statement; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Nvidia said its product roadmap remains intact, in response to SemiAnalysis's reported possibility that the Kyber NVL144 rack system could slip to 2028; the article notes questions linger despite the company's statement.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has come out and said its roadmap remains intact, although questions linger.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e431 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, part of a series of recent product and partnership moves the article lists.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\",\n   \"- e432 [rumour; en.sedaily.com (Lee Seok-jin), citing the semiconductor industry; original source LG Electronics 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute completed development, in the first half of 2026, of an AI server-rack prototype matching the compute-tray specifications of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform. LG plans reliability evaluations in the second half of the year, intends to secure orders from global big tech companies, and plans to begin mass production next year (2027). The report attributes the information to 'the semiconductor industry.'\\n    Quote: LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. … According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans t\",\n   \"- e471 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey, 247wallst.com; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks costs between $6 billion and $11 billion, versus roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks, and that rack costs rise to $25 billion per gigawatt under Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin platform — a third-party cost comparison cited in a 247wallst.com column arguing hyperscaler custom silicon is gaining ground on Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\",\n   \"- e246 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its more powerful Rubin line of processors in the second half of 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e540; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e259 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson (via futuriom.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson responded that \\\"Our road map is intact\\\" and that \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline,\\\" reaffirming the company's schedule in the face of the reported Kyber manufacturing setbacks.\\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e308, e322, e539; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e133 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \\\"manufacturing execution concerns\\\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\",\n   \"- e510 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] 247wallst.com reports that NVIDIA's Blackwell 300 is currently ramping and that the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it, without giving a date or volume for Vera Rubin's launch, shipments or revenue.\\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\",\n   \"- e540 [party_statement; ebc.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, according to the article's account of Nvidia's stated target shipping dates.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e246; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e547 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Under its partnership with Nvidia, Firmus Technologies plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation GPUs in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, to provide AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 2,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $550 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$550 billion to below $600 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $550 billion and less than $600 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$600 billion to below $700 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$800 billion or more\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n   \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be for calendar 2026, as measured by summing the capex lines in their Q4 2026 earnings reports?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' aggregated calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure versus the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item, with Nvidia's own projection of ~$1 trillion for 2027 as the forward reference.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 500000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 500000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\")\",\n    \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"condition\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i232\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Alphabet / Google\",\n     \"what\": \"capital expenditure guidance\",\n     \"how_much\": \"up to $190bn\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i233\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Google Cloud\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth and backlog\",\n     \"how_much\": \"revenue +63%; backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i295\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"title\": \"Here are Friday's biggest analyst calls: Nvidia, SpaceX, Toll, Shopify, Twilio, Seagate, Chipotle & more\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company describing \\\"accelerating growth rates\\\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA management, relayed by Morgan Stanley\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth rates accelerating\",\n     \"how_much\": \"quarterly revenue approaching $100 bn\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e87 [party_statement; fool.com; original source The big four AI hyperscalers (not individually named in the item) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\",\n   \"- e88 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\",\n   \"- e56 [third_party_view; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] The article reports that public opposition to data centers — with 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: This has become a major national issue recently, with data centers being blamed for massive increases in power and utility costs, as well as increased water consumption and a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in th\",\n   \"- e88 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e56 [third_party_view; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] The article reports that public opposition to data centers — with 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: This has become a major national issue recently, with data centers being blamed for massive increases in power and utility costs, as well as increased water consumption and a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in th\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e134 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (management) ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] In its most recent quarterly conference call (the last one before this article), NVIDIA management forecast that AI hyperscalers' data center spending will top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e168 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, Berkshire Money Management (The Berkshire Edge column); original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; autonocion.com citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware (tally and polling, as cited by autonocion.com) ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 US data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the country so far this year, according to a running tally by Tom's Hardware cited in the item, and polling covered in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans do not want a data center built near their home. The item frames this community opposition to power and water demands as a drag on the AI data center buildout.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn't just an engineering story — it's a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e56; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e87 [party_statement; fool.com; original source The big four AI hyperscalers (not individually named in the item) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e212, e218; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e88 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134, e213; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e212 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article, published 2026-07-07; original source The big four AI hyperscalers (Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft) ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The article reports that the 'big four' AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e87; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e213 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article, published 2026-07-07; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the 'big four' AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027).\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e218 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source  2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and per the article that figure could exceed $1 trillion next year (2027).\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e87; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e224 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (reporting Google disclosure); original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditures up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor. Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\",\n   \"- e257 [fact; Bloomberg (via futuriom.com); original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] On July 1, 2026, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models, the same day NVIDIA announced its revenue-sharing model. The item notes Meta had planned earlier in the year to buy 'millions' of NVIDIA GPUs and speculates about what it would mean for AI demand if Meta no longer needs those chips; this demand implication is the article's inference, not a confirmed figure.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\",\n   \"- e262 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Quanta Services (CEO Duke Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services CEO Duke (Earl) Austin put the size of the electrical/grid opportunity tied to AI infrastructure at a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030, describing it as converging utility, generation and large-load demand, as cited in the article.\\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\",\n   \"- e263 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings reported for its March quarter that Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million (attributed to data center demand), Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million, and total backlog rose 62% since the end of fiscal 2025 to $3.86 billion.\\n    Quote: Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million, and total backlog exploded to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025, fueled by customer demand and expansion capacity.\",\n   \"- e264 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus a $2.03 consensus, and a record backlog of $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $34.70–35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55–14.25 in adjusted EPS.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 was a blowout. Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS.\",\n   \"- e265 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year (51% organic), diluted EPS of $10.51 versus a $6.81 consensus, and backlog of $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier.\",\n   \"- e266 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 U.S. Electrical Construction revenue growth of 33.1% and Mechanical Construction growth of 28.8%, diluted EPS of $6.84 versus a $5.90 consensus, record Remaining Performance Obligations of $15.62 billion (up 32.9% year over year), and raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50–19.25 billion in revenue and EPS of $28.25–29.75.\\n    Quote: Its U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while Mechanical Construction added 28.8%. … Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75.\",\n   \"- e267 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 (a double-digit consensus beat), revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin; management raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05 (implying 72% growth at the midpoint).\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\",\n   \"- e279 [third_party_view; Investing.com / Yahoo Finance, reporting Arya's note; original source Bank of America (analyst Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] BofA analyst Vivek Arya said Nvidia's sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice the growth rate of cloud capital expenditure.\\n    Quote: Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e290; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e313 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (management) ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] Nvidia management has stated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\",\n   \"- e350 [rumour; Reuters (as relayed by gurufocus.com); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:09b9d76297a57f198651c5e7 2026-07-10] Reuters reported that Meta Platforms plans to begin manufacturing its custom artificial-intelligence processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom and manufactured by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing, and is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing—rather than replacing—the large number of graphics processors Meta continues to buy from Nvidia and AMD. The item presents this as a reported plan and does not quote Meta confirming it.\\n    Quote: Reuters said Meta plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom (AVGO) and manufactured by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSM). The report said the processor is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics pro\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e335; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e385 [third_party_view; Coinpedia (published via TradingView); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:1bfc0e875ab1f26dcf48fb06 2026-07-11] Morgan Stanley, after meeting with Nvidia's management, reaffirmed its over-weight rating, arguing the AI infrastructure cycle remains in its early innings even though Nvidia's quarterly revenue is approaching the $100 billion milestone; the bank also noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia's demand, with enterprise AI adoption expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley reaffirmed its over-weight rating after meeting with Nvidia’s management, arguing that the AI infrastructure cycle remains in its early innings despite quarterly revenue approaching the $100 billion milestone. The bank noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, highlighting how enterprise AI adoption is expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e379; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e396 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors in its prior quarter (before the article's 2026-07-12 publication) that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (2027).\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\",\n   \"- e406 [party_statement; Nvidia, reported by tech-insider.org; original source AWS (plan stated by Nvidia) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Every HBM stack that goes into a data center accelerator is capacity that does not go into a GeForce card, and Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\",\n   \"- e448 [fact; Danny Vena, Fool.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:3a9054a950c410e249837c91 2026-07-13] Amazon reported Q1 revenue of $182 billion, up 17% year over year, and EPS of $2.78, up 75%; the article highlights reaccelerating cloud growth, with AWS revenue up 28%. Amazon is reported as a major AI/cloud customer of NVIDIA-type compute.\\n    Quote: Yet Amazon’s results continue to impress. In Q1, revenue of $182 billion rose 17% year over year, while EPS of $2.78 jumped 75%. Perhaps more telling is the reacceleration of its cloud growth, as AWS revenue rose 28%.\",\n   \"- e449 [fact; Danny Vena, Fool.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:3a9054a950c410e249837c91 2026-07-13] Microsoft reported fiscal 2026 third-quarter (ended March 31) revenue of $83 billion, up 18% year over year, and diluted EPS of $4.27, up 23%; Azure Cloud revenue jumped 40%. Microsoft is a major AI/cloud customer of NVIDIA-type compute.\\n    Quote: But its financial results tell a different story. In its fiscal 2026 third quarter (ended March 31), Microsoft generated revenue that climbed 18% year over year to $83 billion, while its diluted EPS of $4.27 grew 23%. Perhaps more importantly, its Azure Cloud revenue jumped 40%.\",\n   \"- e450 [fact; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9% year over year, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%; CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking. The article also cites Broadcom's adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%. Broadcom is a competitor of NVIDIA in AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%. CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being “driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.” … Broadcom is compounding a narrow, high-margin ASIC business with an adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e459; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e451 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom management has guided Q3 FY2026 AI revenue to $16.0 billion, roughly 54% of total sales, with its Q3 earnings report scheduled for September 8 (2026), according to the article.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q3 earnings report lands September 8, and management has already telegraphed $16.0 billion in AI revenue, roughly 54% of total sales.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e146; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e470 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.; a hyperscaler customer's capex figure bearing on AI data-center demand.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\",\n   \"- e472 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey, 247wallst.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, after adding roughly 6.7 GW in 2025; Google alone will add 6.8 GW, more than the four hyperscalers added combined two years earlier, according to the 247wallst.com column citing the analyst.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\",\n   \"- e313 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (management) ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] Nvidia management has stated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e478; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e450 [fact; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9% year over year, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%; CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking. The article also cites Broadcom's adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%. Broadcom is a competitor of NVIDIA in AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%. CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being “driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.” … Broadcom is compounding a narrow, high-margin ASIC business with an adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e459, e491, e517; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e477 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool (Justin Pope); original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 ('this year') to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as cited in the article.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\",\n   \"- e498 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Meta ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta has a $145 billion infrastructure budget that is aimed partly at custom silicon, cited among the competitive threats weighing on NVIDIA sentiment.\\n    Quote: The Reddit crowd has fixated on competitive threats, from DeepSeek’s rumored in-house chip to Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\",\n   \"- e515 [fact; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, including Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, up 92% year over year, as cited by 247wallst.com.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s Q1 FY27 revenue hit $81.61B, up 85.2% YoY, with Data Center at $75.25B (+92%).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e452; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e541 [fact; ebc.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon borrowed another $25 billion, a move the article says made headlines and caused a minor selloff in AI-related debt, in the context of Wall Street demanding proof of return on hyperscaler AI capital spending.\\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt . … These tech behemoths have spent hundreds of billions of dollars purchasing Nvidia's hardware, but Wall Street is starting to demand proof of return on investment (ROI).\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 3,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia confirm that it has made first customer shipments of its Kyber NVL144 rack architecture for the Vera Rubin Ultra generation by 2028-12-31, in its quarterly reports or management statements?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"First customer shipments of Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture (Vera Rubin Ultra generation), confirmed by the company, versus the reported more-than-12-month delay to 2028.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2028\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"Nvidia confirms that it has made first customer shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture for the Vera Rubin Ultra generation by 2028-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"After 2026-07-05 and before 2028-12-31, Nvidia formally cancels the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture or formally abandons plans to make first customer shipments of it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2028-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i100\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n     \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n     \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n     \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i179\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n     \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i181\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n     \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\",\n     \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i101\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i197\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n     \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n     \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e96 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of ho\",\n   \"- e97 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis also said that Nvidia's NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n   \"- e98 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. … \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n   \"- e99 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis said that, with the backup design cancelled, Nvidia has \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n   \"- e100 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of the Kyber delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"- e96 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of ho\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113, e129, e174, e178, e182, e187, e189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e97 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis also said that Nvidia's NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e131, e191; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e98 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. … \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114, e130, e175, e180, e190; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e99 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d5