ersistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e115 [party_statement; Nvidia; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's delay claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e99 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis said that, with the backup design cancelled, Nvidia has \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n   \"- e129 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing the release to 2028 from an original 2027 schedule, because the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules remains difficult to manufacture; the Kyber cabinets were designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via Nvidia's NVLink 7 Switch.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. … However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e130 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's alternative plan — placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve similar power output — has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back on its \\\"odd design and heavy operational burden.\\\"\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e98; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e131 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that system will likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, leaving Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.\\\"\\n    Quote: The manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. … All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e97; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e132 [party_statement; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] Asked by DataCenterDynamics for comment on the Kyber claims, an Nvidia spokesperson said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e133 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \\\"manufacturing execution concerns\\\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” … The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e176, e192; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e145 [rumour; Danielle Liverance (247wallst.com); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e174 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis (reported on X), relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically the PCB midplane, a central circuit board connecting the rack's components, proving extremely difficult to produce without defects. Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 three months earlier at GTC.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems. … The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e176 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia canceled the more powerful four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e133; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e177 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next (Feynman), meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the gap could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e178 [rumour; Investing.com / Yahoo Finance; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Supply-chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack system slated to debut with its Vera Rubin Ultra generation, will be delayed to 2028 because of manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane. The claim is an unconfirmed allegation that Nvidia subsequently denied.\\n    Quote: Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e179 [party_statement; Investing.com / Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's delay report, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e133 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \\\"manufacturing execution concerns\\\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\",\n   \"- e180 [rumour; Investing.com / Yahoo Finance; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] In the same weekend report, SemiAnalysis further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design after heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients, and that a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform had scrapped a crucial four-die configuration.\\n    Quote: The research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients. … Additionally, the report pointed to a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e98; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e182 [third_party_view; TIKR.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia’s Kyber next-generation rack-scale server design has been pushed back more than a year, from 2027 to 2028, because of manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system. SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 version, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely to be delayed or to ship only in small volumes.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028. … The design was supposed to launch alongside Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia’s next-generation system, in 2027. The holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis. … A larger version, \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e183 [rumour; SemiAnalysis (via TIKR.com); original source NVIDIA and unnamed cloud customers (per SemiAnalysis) ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia’s backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together to achieve power similar to Rubin Ultra was scrapped after cloud customers reportedly rejected it as awkward and expensive to run. SemiAnalysis said this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now and could give rivals such as AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market.\\n    Quote: Nvidia had a backup plan: bolting two current-generation racks together to achieve similar power, but that idea was scrapped too.\",\n   \"- e187 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, the Kyber NVL144, has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties, specifically setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform; Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment.\\n    Quote: Asian technology stocks slumped after a report that Nvidia’s next-generation AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties. … Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the