{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-1.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",x[\\\"control\\\"]),print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in x.items() if k \"'!= \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\"},ensure_ascii=False)),print(\"EVIDENCE\",len(x[\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\"]))) for x in p]'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"CONTROL 1\n{\"control\": 1, \"ruler_question\": {\"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\", \"unit\": \"yes/no\", \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\", \"rungs\": [{\"label\": \"Yes\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"label\": \"No\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}]}, \"judged_question\": {\"view_id\": \"v4\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia make first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026, as confirmed in Nvidia's quarterly reports, filings, or management commentary?\", \"looks_at\": \"First customer shipments / initial revenue from Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by the company.\", \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\", \"year\": \"2026\", \"kind\": \"asset\"}, \"judged_rungs\": [{\"position\": 1, \"label\": \"Yes\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia confirms that it made first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"position\": 2, \"label\": \"Ruled out\", \"condition\": \"After July 2, 2026, Nvidia formally cancels or abandons the Vera Rubin platform before making any first customer shipments. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"position\": 3, \"label\": \"Still open\", \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}], \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [{\"id\": \"i105\", \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA\", \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\", \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\", \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"}}, {\"id\": \"i219\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\", \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\", \"meaning\": {\"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\", \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\", \"status\": \"plan\"}}, {\"id\": \"i202\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\", \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\", \"period\": \"2026\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, {\"id\": \"i207\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\", \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\", \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\", \"period\": \"this fall\", \"status\": \"happened\", \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"}}]}\nEVIDENCE 37\nCONTROL 2\n{\"control\": 2, \"ruler_question\": {\"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\", \"unit\": \"USD\", \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\", \"rungs\": [{\"label\": \"Below $550 billion\", \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $550 billion.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": 550000000000.0}, {\"label\": \"$550 billion to below $600 billion\", \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $550 billion and less than $600 billion.\", \"lo\": 550000000000.0, \"hi\": 600000000000.0}, {\"label\": \"$600 billion to below $700 billion\", \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion.\", \"lo\": 600000000000.0, \"hi\": 700000000000.0}, {\"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\", \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\", \"lo\": 700000000000.0, \"hi\": 800000000000.0}, {\"label\": \"$800 billion or more\", \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\", \"lo\": 800000000000.0, \"hi\": null}]}, \"judged_question\": {\"view_id\": \"v5\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be for calendar 2026, as measured by summing the capex lines in their Q4 2026 earnings reports?\", \"looks_at\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' aggregated calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure versus the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item, with Nvidia's own projection of ~$1 trillion for 2027 as the forward reference.\", \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\", \"year\": \"2026\", \"kind\": \"theme\"}, \"judged_rungs\": [{\"position\": 1, \"label\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\", \"condition\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": 500000000000.0}, {\"position\": 2, \"label\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\", \"condition\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\", \"lo\": 500000000000.0, \"hi\": 600000000000.0}, {\"position\": 3, \"label\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD\", \"condition\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\")\", \"lo\": 600000000000.0, \"hi\": 700000000000.0}, {\"position\": 4, \"label\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\", \"condition\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\", \"lo\": 700000000000.0, \"hi\": 800000000000.0}, {\"position\": 5, \"label\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\", \"condition\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\", \"lo\": 800000000000.0, \"hi\": null}], \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [{\"id\": \"i256\", \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\", \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\", \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\", \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\", \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\", \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"}}, {\"id\": \"i232\", \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\", \"passage\": \"2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Alphabet / Google\", \"what\": \"capital expenditure guidance\", \"how_much\": \"up to $190bn\", \"period\": \"2026\", \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"}}, {\"id\": \"i233\", \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\", \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Google Cloud\", \"what\": \"revenue growth and backlog\", \"how_much\": \"revenue +63%; backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn\", \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\", \"status\": \"happened\"}}, {\"id\": \"i295\", \"day\": \"2026-07-10\", \"title\": \"Here are Friday's biggest analyst calls: Nvidia, SpaceX, Toll, Shopify, Twilio, Seagate, Chipotle & more\", \"passage\": \"the company describing \\\"accelerating growth rates\\\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA management, relayed by Morgan Stanley\", \"what\": \"revenue growth rates accelerating\", \"how_much\": \"quarterly revenue approaching $100 bn\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}]}\nEVIDENCE 39\nCONTROL 3\n{\"control\": 3, \"ruler_question\": {\"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\", \"unit\": \"yes/no\", \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\", \"rungs\": [{\"label\": \"Yes\", \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"label\": \"No\", \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}]}, \"judged_question\": {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia confirm that it has made first customer shipments of its Kyber NVL144 rack architecture for the Vera Rubin Ultra generation by 2028-12-31, in its quarterly reports or management statements?\", \"looks_at\": \"First customer shipments of Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture (Vera Rubin Ultra generation), confirmed by the company, versus the reported more-than-12-month delay to 2028.\", \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\", \"year\": \"2028\", \"kind\": \"asset\"}, \"judged_rungs\": [{\"position\": 1, \"label\": \"Yes\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia confirms that it has made first customer shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture for the Vera Rubin Ultra generation by 2028-12-31.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"position\": 2, \"label\": \"Ruled out\", \"condition\": \"After 2026-07-05 and before 2028-12-31, Nvidia formally cancels the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture or formally abandons plans to make first customer shipments of it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2028-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"position\": 3, \"label\": \"Still open\", \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-12-31.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}], \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [{\"id\": \"i100\", \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\", \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\", \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\", \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, {\"id\": \"i179\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\", \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\", \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\", \"status\": \"claim\", \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"}}, {\"id\": \"i181\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\", \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\", \"status\": \"plan\", \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"}}, {\"id\": \"i101\", \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA\", \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, {\"id\": \"i197\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\", \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\", \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\", \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}]}\nEVIDENCE 63\nCONTROL 4\n{\"control\": 4, \"ruler_question\": {\"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\", \"unit\": \"yes/no\", \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\", \"rungs\": [{\"label\": \"Yes\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"label\": \"No\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}]}, \"judged_question\": {\"view_id\": \"v2\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Vera Rubin platform by 2026-12-31?\", \"looks_at\": \"Whether Nvidia begins shipping its Vera Rubin platform by the end of 2026.\", \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\", \"year\": \"2026\", \"kind\": \"asset\"}, \"judged_rungs\": [{\"position\": 1, \"label\": \"Yes\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Vera Rubin by 2026-12-31.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}, {\"position\": 2, \"label\": \"No\", \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Vera Rubin by 2026-12-31.\", \"lo\": null, \"hi\": null}], \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [{\"id\": \"i105\", \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA\", \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\", \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\", \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"}}, {\"id\": \"i219\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\", \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\", \"meaning\": {\"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\", \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\", \"status\": \"plan\"}}, {\"id\": \"i202\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\", \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\", \"period\": \"2026\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, {\"id\": \"i207\", \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\", \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\", \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\", \"period\": \"this fall\", \"status\": \"happened\", \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"}}]}\nEVIDENCE 16\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-1.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",x[\\\"control\\\"]),*[print(e) for e in x[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"] if any(w in e.lower() for w in ([\\\"650\\\", \\\"trillion\\\", \\\"190\\\", \\\"460\\\", \\\"63%\\\", \\\"100 bn\\\", \\\"100 billion\\\", \\\"accelerat\\\"] if x[\\\"control\\\"]==2 else [\\\"midplane\\\", \\\"four-die\\\", \\\"four die\\\", \\\"two-die\\\", \\\"two die\\\", \\\"shortfall\\\", \\\"oberon\\\", \\\"roadmap is intact\\\", \\\"roadmap remains intact\\\"]))]) for x in p if x[\\\"control\\\"] in (2,3)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-1.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",x[\\\"control\\\"]),*[print(e) for e in x[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"] if any(w in e.lower() for w in ([\\\"650\\\", \\\"trillion\\\", \\\"190\\\", \\\"460\\\", \\\"63%\\\", \\\"100 bn\\\", \\\"100 billion\\\", \\\"accelerat\\\"] if x[\\\"control\\\"]==2 else [\\\"midplane\\\", \\\"four-die\\\", \\\"four die\\\", \\\"two-die\\\", \\\"two die\\\", \\\"shortfall\\\", \\\"oberon\\\", \\\"roadmap is intact\\\", \\\"roadmap remains intact\\\"]))]) for x in p if x[\\\"control\\\"] in (2,3)]'\"","aggregated_output":"CONTROL 2\n- e87 [party_statement; fool.com; original source The big four AI hyperscalers (not individually named in the item) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\n- e88 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\n- e88 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e134 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (management) ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] In its most recent quarterly conference call (the last one before this article), NVIDIA management forecast that AI hyperscalers' data center spending will top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e168 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, Berkshire Money Management (The Berkshire Edge column); original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\n- e87 [party_statement; fool.com; original source The big four AI hyperscalers (not individually named in the item) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e212, e218; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e88 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134, e213; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e212 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article, published 2026-07-07; original source The big four AI hyperscalers (Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft) ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The article reports that the 'big four' AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e87; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e213 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article, published 2026-07-07; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the 'big four' AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027).\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e218 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source  2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and per the article that figure could exceed $1 trillion next year (2027).\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e87; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e224 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (reporting Google disclosure); original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditures up to $190 billion.\n    Quote: Google ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor. Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\n- e262 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Quanta Services (CEO Duke Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services CEO Duke (Earl) Austin put the size of the electrical/grid opportunity tied to AI infrastructure at a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030, describing it as converging utility, generation and large-load demand, as cited in the article.\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\n- e313 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (management) ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] Nvidia management has stated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, according to the article.\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n- e385 [third_party_view; Coinpedia (published via TradingView); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:1bfc0e875ab1f26dcf48fb06 2026-07-11] Morgan Stanley, after meeting with Nvidia's management, reaffirmed its over-weight rating, arguing the AI infrastructure cycle remains in its early innings even though Nvidia's quarterly revenue is approaching the $100 billion milestone; the bank also noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia's demand, with enterprise AI adoption expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley reaffirmed its over-weight rating after meeting with Nvidia’s management, arguing that the AI infrastructure cycle remains in its early innings despite quarterly revenue approaching the $100 billion milestone. The bank noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, highlighting how enterprise AI adoption is expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e379; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e396 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors in its prior quarter (before the article's 2026-07-12 publication) that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (2027).\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\n- e406 [party_statement; Nvidia, reported by tech-insider.org; original source AWS (plan stated by Nvidia) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\n    Quote: Every HBM stack that goes into a data center accelerator is capacity that does not go into a GeForce card, and Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\n- e448 [fact; Danny Vena, Fool.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:3a9054a950c410e249837c91 2026-07-13] Amazon reported Q1 revenue of $182 billion, up 17% year over year, and EPS of $2.78, up 75%; the article highlights reaccelerating cloud growth, with AWS revenue up 28%. Amazon is reported as a major AI/cloud customer of NVIDIA-type compute.\n    Quote: Yet Amazon’s results continue to impress. In Q1, revenue of $182 billion rose 17% year over year, while EPS of $2.78 jumped 75%. Perhaps more telling is the reacceleration of its cloud growth, as AWS revenue rose 28%.\n- e450 [fact; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9% year over year, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%; CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking. The article also cites Broadcom's adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%. Broadcom is a competitor of NVIDIA in AI accelerators.\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%. CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being “driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.” … Broadcom is compounding a narrow, high-margin ASIC business with an adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e459; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e472 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey, 247wallst.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, after adding roughly 6.7 GW in 2025; Google alone will add 6.8 GW, more than the four hyperscalers added combined two years earlier, according to the 247wallst.com column citing the analyst.\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\n- e313 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (management) ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] Nvidia management has stated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, according to the article.\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e478; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e450 [fact; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9% year over year, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%; CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking. The article also cites Broadcom's adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%. Broadcom is a competitor of NVIDIA in AI accelerators.\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%. CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being “driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.” … Broadcom is compounding a narrow, high-margin ASIC business with an adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e459, e491, e517; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e477 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool (Justin Pope); original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 ('this year') to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as cited in the article.\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\nCONTROL 3\n- e96 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of ho\n- e100 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of the Kyber delay, stating that \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n- e96 [third_party_view; CNBC, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of ho\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113, e129, e174, e178, e182, e187, e189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e100 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of the Kyber delay, stating that \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e115, e132, e179, e193; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e113 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis claimed that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 high-density rack architecture, built for Rubin Ultra GPUs and showcased at GTC earlier in 2026, has slipped by more than 12 months, blaming persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\n    Quote: The clash pits Nvidia against research firm SemiAnalysis, which alleges manufacturing setbacks have hit the Kyber NVL144 architecture showcased at GTC earlier this year. … SemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e115 [party_statement; Nvidia; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's delay claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e99 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis said that, with the backup design cancelled, Nvidia has \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" and predicted that this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\n- e129 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing the release to 2028 from an original 2027 schedule, because the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules remains difficult to manufacture; the Kyber cabinets were designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via Nvidia's NVLink 7 Switch.\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. … However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the p\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e130 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's alternative plan — placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve similar power output — has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back on its \"odd design and heavy operational burden.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e98; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e131 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that system will likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, leaving Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.\"\n    Quote: The manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. … All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e97; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e132 [party_statement; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] Asked by DataCenterDynamics for comment on the Kyber claims, an Nvidia spokesperson said: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e133 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \"manufacturing execution concerns\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” … The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e176, e192; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e174 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis (reported on X), relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically the PCB midplane, a central circuit board connecting the rack's components, proving extremely difficult to produce without defects. Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 three months earlier at GTC.\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems. … The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e176 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia canceled the more powerful four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e133; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e177 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next (Feynman), meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the gap could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e178 [rumour; Investing.com / Yahoo Finance; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Supply-chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack system slated to debut with its Vera Rubin Ultra generation, will be delayed to 2028 because of manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane. The claim is an unconfirmed allegation that Nvidia subsequently denied.\n    Quote: Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e179 [party_statement; Investing.com / Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's delay report, saying: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e133 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported about a week earlier that Nvidia had cancelled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favour of a two-chiplet design, also citing \"manufacturing execution concerns\"; the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die 