 (Luke James); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to SemiAnalysis, Nvidia killed the NVL72x2 stopgap rack — which would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink — after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. The report says this leaves no proven alternative to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\n    Quote: Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027. … NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running t\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e98; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e193 [party_statement; Nvidia representative (via Tom's Hardware); original source Nvidia 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An Nvidia representative responded to Tom's Hardware with the short statement \"Our roadmap is intact,\" providing no further detail; the outlet notes it is unclear whether this refers to the original roadmap and delivery schedules or to an already-updated one.\n    Quote: An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \"Our roadmap is intact.\" … Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions. As such, it is unclear if Nvidia's statement refers to its original roadmap, planned delivery schedules, and hardware, or if those particulars had already been changed and the statement refers to a newly updated roadmap.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e100; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e194 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source SemiA